22:20:32 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Shared experience can preserve useful lessons—such as staying alert in unfamiliar situations or avoiding people who are acting threateningly. But traditions can also preserve fears that were shaped by particular historical events, selective stories, media coverage, or prejudice. The fact that an idea has been passed down does not make it a reliable rule for judging an individual today. A useful question is: **What exactly was the original evidence, and does it justify a general rule about every person in a religion?** Families often pass along vivid anecdotes and warnings, while harmless day-to-day encounters are not remembered or retold. That can make a rare danger feel much more common than it is. You do not have to dismiss your family or pretend threats never exist. You can keep the part of the lesson that protects people: - Pay attention to your surroundings. - Leave or seek help when someone behaves aggressively or makes you uncomfortable. - Take direct threats and credible warnings seriously. - Teach children practical safety skills and boundaries. But replace the religion-based shortcut with behavior-based judgment. A Muslim person who is simply going about their day is not giving you evidence of danger. A person of any background who threatens, stalks, harasses, or acts violently is. That approach respects the underlying goal—keeping yourself and family safe—while avoiding a rule that will mostly misidentify harmless people and can unfairly harm them.
