22:21:01 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Neither extreme. There is a real, visible pattern: some of the most widely covered terrorist attacks have been committed by groups or individuals using Islamist extremist ideology. News outlets report those attacks because they are consequential, and your family’s concern may reflect genuine exposure to frightening events. But people can also draw an overly broad conclusion from a real pattern. Media coverage, repeated images, and family stories can make one category feel like the main or only danger, even when: - terrorism is rare relative to ordinary day-to-day risks; - attacks arise from multiple ideologies and motives; - global totals are heavily shaped by a small number of conflict zones and organized groups; and - the overwhelming majority of Muslims have no involvement in, and reject, extremist violence. So nobody has to be “duped.” It is a normal human tendency to use memorable, emotionally intense examples as shortcuts for risk. The correction is not to pretend the attacks did not happen; it is to avoid treating a broad religious identity as if it were evidence about the intentions of a particular person. A defensible rule is: take concrete threats, extremist advocacy, and credible intelligence seriously—regardless of who they involve—but do not treat being Muslim, by itself, as a reason to distrust someone.
