22:21:17 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You do not have to choose between “elites” and “ordinary people” to use a fair safety standard. Regular working people have good reasons to care about safety, notice patterns, and distrust simplistic reassurances. At the same time, firsthand experience is limited: most people do not personally encounter terrorism, while they repeatedly encounter coverage and conversation about it. That is true for academics, journalists, and everyone else. The practical question is not who belongs to the more trustworthy social group. It is whether a rule helps you make better decisions: - “Be alert to threatening behavior, credible warnings, and specific risks” is useful. - “Assume someone is risky because they are Muslim” is not a reliable day-to-day safety rule. The first protects you from people of any background who actually show danger signs. The second mainly creates suspicion toward people who have done nothing wrong, and may cause you to overlook risks that come from someone who does not fit the category you expect. There are also many ordinary Muslim working people—coworkers, shop owners, nurses, teachers, drivers, parents—whose normal lives are not represented by terrorism coverage. They are not a separate security category from other people in your community. You can reject violent Islamist ideology, support effective counterterrorism, and care about your family’s safety without making ordinary Muslims carry suspicion for crimes they did not commit.
