22:24:02 | INFO | TargetAgent (gpt-5.6-terra) cache: hit=7548 miss=268
22:24:02 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Religion can be relevant to understanding the ideology of some terrorist movements. It is not accurate, though, to say it is the common thread in “basically every” major attack of the last twenty years. Major attacks have also been driven by far-right racial ideology, ethno-nationalism, anti-government extremism, sectarian nationalism, political insurgency, and personal grievances—for example, Anders Breivik in Norway, the Christchurch mosque attack, the Pittsburgh synagogue shooting, the Oklahoma City bombing, and numerous attacks tied to separatist or nationalist conflicts. More precisely: **Islamist extremist ideology has been a major source of terrorism in certain eras and conflict zones.** That is a real security fact. But “Islam” is not a sufficiently specific explanation on its own. The relevant distinction is between: - a person or group expressing support for violent Islamist ideology, ISIS, al-Qaeda, or a concrete violent cause; and - a person who is Muslim, with no evidence of violent beliefs or conduct. Those are very different pieces of information. The first can be highly relevant to risk; the second, by itself, is not. That is not saying religion “literally tells us nothing.” It is saying religious identity is too broad to stand in for violent intent.
