22:24:37 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You do not have to give up your desire for safety or ignore an uneasy feeling. The useful distinction is between a feeling and an action: - A gut reaction can be a cue to stay aware of your surroundings. - It should not, by itself, become a conclusion that a person is dangerous because they are Muslim. A broad stereotype may feel reassuring because it gives uncertainty a simple rule. But it can produce false reassurance too: you may relax around someone showing real warning signs because they do not fit the stereotype, while becoming guarded around a harmless person whose only “signal” is their faith. A better peace-of-mind rule is simple, not exhausting: use the same ordinary safety habits with everyone, and increase caution only when there is something concrete—aggression, threats, harassment, intoxication, attempts to isolate you, a credible warning, or a known violent affiliation. That protects your autonomy and your family without asking you to perform constant analysis or to treat strangers as responsible for crimes committed by unrelated extremists.
